Lockport N.Y. Feb 4, 1890
Hon. J.C. Pilling;
My Dear Sir;
I send you 12 additional pages of my new hymn book which I am pushing to completion as rapidly as possible. I hope to finish the book this spring or summer. I will send you a few copies when completed. I thought

camley had a narative of his situation from the traders wife, and then told the boy that he must be adopted into his tribe, and become an Indian, if he would save his life;
That there was no other way in which his life could be saved.
To that end, the chief adopted him into his own familey, and told Joseph that he was his uncle and that Kiachatalee was his brother. His head was then shaved, leaving onley a fillet of hair on the top, in which a bunch of feathers was tied, his ears pirced for rings, and his clothes taken off; The flaps substituted for trousers, and a short shirt substituted for a coat, shirt, and vest and his nether vestments

20
The next day, the 9th of May, the solitary perogue or flatboat of the pioneer Brown had passed several Indian villages, and had come in view of the town of Running Water and Nickajack, the last Cherokee towns where, there was any considerable body of Indians.
The voyagers began to rejoice in their happy deliverance from the principal dangers which had threatened their journey. They would in a few hours be through the pases of mountains, on the wide bosom of a noble river, where they would be comparatively free from ambuskades of lurking Indians. But suddenly four canoes, with white flags raised, and naked savages kneeling in them as rowers, glided out into the river and rapidley approached;

"Sometimes we see a cloud that's dragonish;
A vapor sometimes like a bear or lion;
A flock of goats, half sleeping and half walking,
Or cattle grazing by the lofty cliffs;
A towered citadel, or pendant rock;
A vessel sailing on a golden sea;
Or scarlet robes gracing the lofty hill tops;
A forked mountain or blue promontory,
With trees upon't that nod unto the world,
And mock our eyes with sights aerial;
Bright pageants these, of slow approaching
And striking emblems of earth's fading joys."
